Database Management Systems (DBMSs) serve as the backbone for efficient data access and modification through the Structured Query Language (SQL). Bugs in DBMS implementations may prevent otherwise valid statements from executing or even cause statements to return incorrect results. Prior research has primarily focused on detecting bugs in ordinary SQL statements. In contrast, prepared statements—a language feature widely used in production environments to improve the performance of repeated queries and to guard against SQL injection—have received far less attention, and the potential bugs within them remain insufficiently explored.
 
